Rivet creates projects and runs their native tools from Neovim. Start with one language and one successful run; graphics SDKs, modules, and cluster hooks can come afterward. The CLI is optional.

## Install in Neovim

Use Neovim 0.11+ on macOS or Linux. Your plugin manager downloads Rivet; you do not need to clone the repository manually or compile the plugin.

With Neovim 0.12+'s built-in package manager, add this to your configuration:

```lua
vim.pack.add({
  {
    src = 'https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet',
    name = 'rivet.nvim',
    version = vim.version.range('0.1.x'),
  },
})
require('rivet').setup()
```

With lazy.nvim on Neovim 0.11+, use this plugin specification:

```lua
{
  'guzman109/Rivet',
  name = 'rivet.nvim',
  main = 'rivet',
  version = '0.1.x',
  opts = {},
}
```

Restart Neovim after adding the plugin to your configuration. The version range follows patch releases in the 0.1 series. Both managers store the installed revision in a lockfile.

To pin one release, replace the range with `version = 'v0.1.0'` for `vim.pack`, or use `tag = 'v0.1.0', version = false` for lazy.nvim. To follow development, use `version = 'main'` for `vim.pack`, or `branch = 'main', version = false` for lazy.nvim. Changing this selection for an already installed plugin also requires the update operation below; restarting alone does not change its installed revision. See [Neovim package specifications](https://neovim.io/doc/user/pack.html#vim.pack.Spec) and [lazy.nvim version selection](https://lazy.folke.io/spec#spec-versioning).

## Update the plugin

For `vim.pack`, run `:lua vim.pack.update({ 'rivet.nvim' })`, review the proposed update, save it with `:write`, then restart Neovim. For lazy.nvim, use `:Lazy update rivet.nvim`. Publication of a release does not automatically change an installed plugin.

After updating Rivet, review `:RivetUpgrade` separately in each generated project. That command updates supplementary templates while preserving local edits; it does not update the plugin itself. See [project upgrades](/docs/upgrading/) and the [changelog](https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md).

## Local development

To work on the plugin itself, clone the repository and add the checkout to the runtime path:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet.git rivet.nvim
```

```lua
vim.opt.runtimepath:prepend('/absolute/path/to/rivet.nvim')
require('rivet').setup()
```

For the optional CLI, download and extract the source archive from [GitHub releases](https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet/releases), then add its `bin/` directory to your `PATH`. The launcher is `bin/rivet`; it needs Lua 5.1+ or LuaJIT. The archive contains portable source, not a compiled standalone binary. Keep the extracted folder intact so the launcher can find its Lua modules.

Run `:RivetDoctor`. If Neovim says the command does not exist, check the checkout path and restart after loading the configuration. No separate Lua installation is needed inside Neovim. The [Neovim guide](/docs/neovim/) covers keys, commands, and previews; the [CLI quick start](/docs/getting-started/) explains the optional terminal interface.

## Choose your first project

| Language | Tools for the first run | Follow next |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C++ | CMake 3.30+, Ninja 1.11+, compiler supporting your chosen standard; modules also need compatible dependency scanning | [C++ first project](/docs/first-project/) |
| C | CMake 3.30+, Ninja, C compiler supporting C17 (default), C11, or C23 | [Your first C project](/docs/first-c-project/) |
| Python | Python 3.11+; uv only if you choose it | [Your first Python project](/docs/first-python-project/) |
| Rust | Cargo and rustc supporting edition 2024 (default) or 2021 | [Your first Rust project](/docs/first-rust-project/) |
| Lua | Lua 5.1+ or LuaJIT for portable apps; Neovim for plugin projects | [Your first Lua project](/docs/first-lua-project/) |
| Swift | Swift 6.0+ with SwiftPM; Xcode for Apple application work | [Your first Swift project](/docs/first-swift-project/) |

C++26 remains the default. The C++ tutorial uses named modules; conventional headers are also supported in C++17/20/23/26. For a simpler first build without module scanning, choose **Headers and source files** or use the [C++ CLI example](/docs/getting-started/#create-a-first-project).

Each walkthrough creates a project in a folder you choose, explains the expected result, and shows how to run its test. Doctor reports checks it can perform; a successful build and test verifies the actual workflow. See [Compatibility](/docs/compatibility/) for tested combinations and [Doctor coverage](/docs/troubleshooting/#what-doctor-checks) for its limits.

## Already have a project?

Open a file inside your existing CMake, Python, Cargo, SwiftPM, or configured Lua project and use `:RivetTask`. You do not need to create a Rivet manifest to use its native tasks. Follow [Existing projects](/docs/existing-projects/) for discovery and target selection.

For your next step, use [parallel computing and CUDA](/docs/hpc/) or [graphics profiles](/docs/graphics/). Those guides keep SDK setup, compilation, and runtime validation separate.

## Document your first API

For C/C++, choose **Doxygen documentation** while personalizing file headers. Add documentation to public declarations as you add code, then generate HTML for readers and XML for documentation tools. The [Doxygen guide](/docs/doxygen/) explains the workflow and the language adapters needed outside Doxygen’s native support. Each first-project walkthrough includes its language’s documentation path.
